  "detail": "The run ended after 5 rounds, and its last in-run finding is still open. A credential's last line leaks when a hunk is clipped inside the credential at end of file with no trailing newline. `privatePatchLines` (about 90 lines) reconstructs sides per hunk and matches spans, clipped prefixes and suffixes, and adjacent pairs, and each round has found another hunk-boundary case. Patches are the wrong place to look for a multiline value, because a hunk can show any slice of it. Replace the heuristic with a whole-file check. For the selected file, read its full content at the pinned base and the pinned head with the hardened git wrapper (`git show <sha>:<path>`, with the same literal-path handling already used for the diff). If either side contains any multiline registered credential or multiline private string, return no patch text for that file: `[withheld: private text; 1 file]`. Compare after the same normalization the per-line filter uses (NFKC, lowercase, and CRLF/CR as LF). Otherwise use the existing per-line `privacy()` filter unchanged. Hunk headers, function annotations, clipped hunks and missing trailing newlines then need no special handling, because the file is withheld as a whole. Remove `privatePatchLines`. Keep tests that cover: an added file with a two-line credential; a modified file whose hunk is clipped inside the credential at end of file with no trailing newline (the current failing case); a deleted file containing one; a CRLF variant; and a file with no multiline value whose diff stays fully visible. Each must fail on today's main. Budget: about 40 source lines."
 }
